  ImageSizeRaw->{640., 800.},
  PlotRange->{{0, 640.}, {0, 800.}}]], "Output",
 TextAlignment->Center,ExpressionUUID->"420afd14-4e0a-0042-8465-d3caa839cc10"],

Cell["\<\
Figure 1. WolframGuard sits inside the server. The MCP client approval can be \
removed by always-allow (shown dashed); the OS read-only lock cannot be, and \
it denies the write even when the classifier does not.\
\>", "Caption",ExpressionUUID->"dfcf31b5-b275-eb4f-8d32-346c042b518f"],

Cell["Findings at a glance", "Section",ExpressionUUID->"89910ef0-36b2-6c48-ae11-9882da7aad80"],

Cell["\<\
Everything below is measured against the real Wolfram/AgentTools 2.2.0 \
evaluator, with synthetic data only, an offline kernel, and before-and-after \
state checked independently of anything the guard reports about itself.\
\>", "Text",ExpressionUUID->"bd5c9aa8-8781-0642-8429-569b1ed4a92c"],

Cell["\<\
The evaluator has no gate of its own. Driven directly over MCP, it ran DELETE \
FROM customers WHERE status='inactive' and removed 250 synthetic rows with no \
prompt and no confirmation.\
\>", "Item",ExpressionUUID->"25c5cd3d-9571-df46-87d4-4ac74d52d292"],

Cell["\<\
The only real protection is the client's per-tool approval, and the vendor's \
own documentation names that click as the mitigation while warning about \
prompt injection. One always-allow removes it, the paclet ships no \
server-side policy, and the default local evaluation method is the one \
Wolfram's docs label not sandboxed.\
\>", "Item",ExpressionUUID->"0aa0a60b-8990-2d44-9abe-48d39e5d04f8"],

Cell["\<\
A denylist is the wrong foundation. Deciding whether arbitrary code is safe \
by inspection is undecidable in general, and the evasion battery found a \
genuine head-only aliasing bypass in our own classifier, since fixed.\
\>", "Item",ExpressionUUID->"5d6c69a6-030d-2041-ab00-983a19f1e412"],

Cell["\<\
A Wolfram sandbox misses database writes that an OS lock catches. \
Developer`StartProtectedMode contains Wolfram-level file and process effects \
and cannot be escaped from inside, but it does not contain JDBC database \
writes, because the Java runtime writes the file directly. An OS deny-write \
lock contains both.\
\>", "Item",ExpressionUUID->"c7fb5ded-1fc6-6d4a-a071-6c8d8882e159"],

Cell["\<\
Rebuilt on containment, the guarantee holds with the denylist switched off. \
Across 19 evasion payloads, Off changed 16 of 19 fixtures, ReadOnly changed \
none, and ContainmentOnly, which disables the classifier and allows every \
payload, also changed none.\
\>", "Item",ExpressionUUID->"7f324a4b-7cf2-f747-84cc-49c1d5edd13b"],

Cell["\<\
Reads are not collateral damage. Under the same lock a legitimate SELECT \
count of inactive rows returns 250 while a DELETE on the same connection \
fails.\
\>", "Item",ExpressionUUID->"9dbe9c2d-a5c9-4341-8493-a29fa0e9f9af"],

Cell["\<\
The honest residual. The lock is a same-user ACL, so full robustness wants a \
lower-privileged principal, and it confines writes rather than reads.\
\>", "Item",ExpressionUUID->"808dd2ae-98db-034a-9483-23c81a2ea2bc"],

Cell["\<\
In one sentence: on a default AgentTools install an injected DELETE meets \
exactly one obstacle, a checkbox in the chat client, so WolframGuard adds a \
second line inside the server that stops the write at the operating system, \
proven by disabling our own classifier and watching all 19 evasion payloads \
still change nothing.\
\>", "Text",ExpressionUUID->"88bf710c-bd0f-c748-bfa0-1a848fad566a"],

Cell["The problem", "Section",ExpressionUUID->"3db0bf25-e32e-4d44-9515-be3e9aa97b53"],

Cell["\<\
The most powerful tool you can hand an AI agent is a general-purpose code \
evaluator, and Wolfram ships one. Wolfram/AgentTools is an MCP server whose \
WolframLanguageEvaluator tool takes a string of Wolfram Language from the \
model and runs it in a live kernel, able to read and write files, open \
databases, spawn processes and reach the network, all with the launching \
user's full rights. That power is the product. It is also the attack surface.\
\
\>", "Text",ExpressionUUID->"f09e15ad-b85e-d34a-b59f-9d98c240439f"],

Cell["\<\
The standard risk for any tool-using agent is prompt injection. The model \
reads some text, a document, a web page, a returned tool result, and that \
text carries instructions it cannot reliably tell apart from the user's own. \
When the tool in question is run this arbitrary code, a single successful \
injection reaches the whole machine.\
\>", "Text",ExpressionUUID->"bed6887a-352e-a741-a466-55795f83ea47"],

Cell["\<\
This is not our framing. It is the vendor's. The AgentTools documentation has \
a Security Considerations section stating plainly that the evaluator executes \
arbitrary Wolfram Language code, and it lists the consequences: reading, \
writing and deleting files, making network requests, running system commands, \
and reading environment variables. A companion note on prompt injection warns \
that untrusted content could steer the agent into misusing its tools. The \
revealing part is the mitigation it recommends, which is client-side approval \
permissions. In effect the vendor's entire recommended defense for a tool \
that runs arbitrary code is the human clicking approve in the chat client.\
\>", "Text",ExpressionUUID->"5e378fce-661f-e544-a24a-e6703d0dea6a"],

Cell["\<\
We measured what that leaves. Driven directly over MCP, the evaluator deleted \
250 synthetic rows with no prompt, so it has no gate of its own. Behind \
Claude Code at default permissions every model-requested Wolfram call was \
denied before reaching the server, which is good, but that protection lives \
entirely in the client, it is coarse because it decides about the tool rather \
than the code, and it disappears the moment a user clicks always allow. There \
is no fallback beneath it, because the paclet ships no server-side policy and \
the default local evaluation method is the one its own documentation calls \
not sandboxed.\
\>", "Text",ExpressionUUID->"65b619b3-ecae-5f4a-8da0-56ee09c5f775"],

Cell["How peers police unsafe code", "Section",ExpressionUUID->"3caef9a5-507c-6046-8558-4bec211f59d2"],

Cell["\<\
Serious code-execution tools do not rely on the model behaving. They use four \
mechanisms, and the useful question is where each one lives and whether it \
survives this particular setup, a third-party MCP server exposing an \
arbitrary-code tool behind a client the user may have told to always allow.\
\>", "Text",ExpressionUUID->"209dfee2-91de-bc47-8f81-304a8925ab02"],

Cell[BoxData[
 TagBox[GridBox[{
    {
     StyleBox["\"\<Mechanism\>\"",
      StripOnInput->False,
      FontWeight->Bold], 
     StyleBox["\"\<Example\>\"",
      StripOnInput->False,
      FontWeight->Bold], 
     StyleBox["\"\<Lives in\>\"",
      StripOnInput->False,
      FontWeight->Bold], 
     StyleBox["\"\<Survives always-allow?\>\"",
      StripOnInput->False,
      FontWeight->Bold]},
    {"\"\<Model refusal\>\"", "\"\<any trained model\>\"", "\"\<the \
model\>\"", "\"\<no, defeated by injection\>\""},
    {"\"\<Harness content analysis\>\"", "\"\<Claude Code Bash injection \
detection\>\"", "\"\<the client\>\"", "\"\<only its own shell\>\""},
    {"\"\<Capability / approval gating\>\"", "\"\<MCP per-tool approval, \
Codex policy\>\"", "\"\<the client\>\"", "\"\<no, this is the click removed\>\
\""},
    {"\"\<OS / runtime sandbox\>\"", "\"\<Claude Code /sandbox, Codex, \
Wolfram Local/Cloud\>\"", "\"\<OS / runtime\>\"", "\"\<yes when enabled, off \
here\>\""}
   },
   AutoDelete->False,
   BaseStyle->{FontSize -> 11},
   GridBoxAlignment->{"Columns" -> {{Left}}},
   GridBoxBackground->{
    "Columns" -> {{None}}, "RowsIndexed" -> {1 -> GrayLevel[0.92]}},
   GridBoxFrame->{"Columns" -> {{True}}, "Rows" -> {{True}}},
   GridBoxItemSize->{"Columns" -> {{Automatic}}, "Rows" -> {{Automatic}}},
   GridBoxSpacings->{"Columns" -> {{2}}, "Rows" -> {{1}}}],
  "Grid"]], "Text",ExpressionUUID->"1deccba6-3877-6644-9c0f-8b4c074587aa"],

Cell["\<\
Mainstream agents put their unsafe-code policy in the harness and the \
operating system, and usually turn it on by default. A default local \
AgentTools install has neither on, and the one client that analyzes code \
content analyzes its own shell rather than a third-party evaluator's argument \
string. WolframGuard fills the empty slot with a task-bound, server-side \
layer that neither the model nor a convenience click can switch off.\
\>", "Text",ExpressionUUID->"8dbfeea2-e796-334f-a045-a61a83795edc"],

Cell["The idea: containment, not a list", "Section",ExpressionUUID->"bf76eb76-39fd-2d45-80ad-cf05e3ed0162"],

Cell["\<\
The weak way to defend a code evaluator is a denylist: enumerate the \
dangerous primitives, DeleteFile, RunProcess, an SQL DELETE, and refuse them. \
It is weak for a reason that no amount of list-tending repairs. Deciding \
whether arbitrary code is safe by looking at it is undecidable in general, \
and trivially so once the code can build a string and evaluate it. A denylist \
is a list of the attacks you already thought of, and the first primitive you \
did not list walks straight through.\
\>", "Text",ExpressionUUID->"cb35c8d9-5cd6-0a45-ade4-6337366a8f38"],

Cell["\<\
So WolframGuard inverts the question. For a read-only task the code's \
legitimate needs are small and knowable, compute, read files, run SELECT, so \
the robust posture is default-deny on effects: permit that small set and make \
everything else fail by construction. The way to enforce default-deny is not \
a cleverer scan but capability containment, putting the evaluation where the \
write cannot happen. WolframGuard applies an operating-system read-only lock \
to the authorized data, so a write fails at the OS regardless of which \
primitive or language issues it.\
\>", "Text",ExpressionUUID->"53b80a27-ef7b-bc49-be39-555f46176ff6"],

Cell["\<\
The effect classifier from the first version survives, demoted to a fast \
pre-filter. It rejects obvious danger early with a clean message, logs every \
call, and blocks the process and network axes so the lock cannot be tampered \
with. It is no longer what the guarantee rests on, and the containment proof \
below shows that by switching it off entirely and watching the data hold.\
\>", "Text",ExpressionUUID->"b441be2b-419d-be44-9562-b92e32d9d31f"],

Cell["Why the OS lock and not a Wolfram sandbox", "Section",ExpressionUUID->"ba1ffa4d-91a0-ff4f-a2ca-c0aa467e8c56"],

Cell["\<\
Wolfram ships an in-kernel sandbox, Developer`StartProtectedMode, which the \
Local evaluation method uses. We measured what it actually contains on this \
machine before trusting it.\
\>", "Text",ExpressionUUID->"1f1adfdc-2bf9-c14a-8100-2f2fd86b8d64"],

Cell[BoxData[
 TagBox[GridBox[{
    {
     StyleBox["\"\<Attack (arbitrary code)\>\"",
      StripOnInput->False,
      FontWeight->Bold], 
     StyleBox["\"\<StartProtectedMode\>\"",
      StripOnInput->False,
      FontWeight->Bold], 
     StyleBox["\"\<OS deny-write lock\>\"",
      StripOnInput->False,
      FontWeight->Bold]},
    {"\"\<Wolfram DeleteFile or OpenWrite\>\"", "\"\<contained\>\"", \
"\"\<contained\>\""},
    {"\"\<RunProcess\>\"", "\"\<contained\>\"", "\"\<not applicable, no data \
write\>\""},
    {"\"\<JDBC DELETE (DatabaseLink)\>\"", "\"\<NOT contained, DB 250 to \
0\>\"", "\"\<contained, stays 250\>\""},
    {"\"\<escape via EndProtectedMode\>\"", "\"\<inert, cannot escape\>\"", \
"\"\<not applicable\>\""}
   },
   AutoDelete->False,
   BaseStyle->{FontSize -> 11},
   GridBoxAlignment->{"Columns" -> {{Left}}},
   GridBoxBackground->{
    "Columns" -> {{None}}, "RowsIndexed" -> {1 -> GrayLevel[0.92]}},
   GridBoxFrame->{"Columns" -> {{True}}, "Rows" -> {{True}}},
   GridBoxItemSize->{"Columns" -> {{Automatic}}, "Rows" -> {{Automatic}}},
   GridBoxSpacings->{"Columns" -> {{2}}, "Rows" -> {{1}}}],
  "Grid"]], "Text",ExpressionUUID->"0abedc5b-8afd-c944-90c8-027a35b98442"],

Cell["\<\
The database row is the whole point. A JDBC write goes through the Java \
runtime and the SQLite driver, outside Wolfram's own file guard, so a \
Wolfram-level sandbox misses the headline database attack. The OS lock \
catches it because the operating system does not care which runtime asks. \
That is why the guarantee rests on the OS lock, with protected mode as an \
in-kernel complement rather than the other way around.\
\>", "Text",ExpressionUUID->"86ea040c-2762-a24f-9324-0d87b6d763df"],

Cell["How it works", "Section",ExpressionUUID->"c3522b70-40a8-4a4f-95a2-d2559c83a04b"],

Cell["\<\
WolframGuard is two small files and no change to the installed paclet, which \
is deliberate: a defense you can place in front of software you do not own is \
a defense people can actually adopt.\
\>", "Text",ExpressionUUID->"ea2b8ce2-d0dc-8e42-b900-6de05dfc684d"],

Cell["\<\
guarded_server.wl is the launcher. It installs the gate, applies the \
containment lock, and then starts the real AgentTools server, failing closed \
if the guard file is missing. For every policy except Off it reads the \
authorized task paths from the environment and applies an OS deny-write ACL \
for the current user, covering writes, creation, deletion, rename and \
attribute changes. The evaluator runs as that user, so its writes to the task \
data are refused by the operating system before any Wolfram or Java code gets \
to try.\
\>", "Item",ExpressionUUID->"4fd63272-5127-de4f-993c-3c7d80961976"],

Cell["\<\
WolframGuard.wl is the pre-filter. It wraps the single evaluation entry point \
every call funnels through and classifies each one before it runs, parsing \
the string into an inert held expression it never releases, then reading the \
symbols and string literals into effect categories: filesystem write, \
process, network, database mutation, dynamic evaluation, and parse failure, \
which fails closed. SELECT reads and pure computation pass.\
\>", "Item",ExpressionUUID->"85f01f7e-1b51-3642-83b0-fd8b78ebea8c"],

Cell["\<\
Policies are read once from the environment at startup, and the model can \
change neither the policy nor the locked paths.\
\>", "Text",ExpressionUUID->"f0b9f908-55bc-3346-9ea8-9097242bab47"],

Cell[BoxData[
 TagBox[GridBox[{
    {
     StyleBox["\"\<Policy\>\"",
      StripOnInput->False,
      FontWeight->Bold], 
     StyleBox["\"\<OS lock\>\"",
      StripOnInput->False,
      FontWeight->Bold], 
     StyleBox["\"\<Classifier\>\"",
      StripOnInput->False,
      FontWeight->Bold], 
     StyleBox["\"\<State-changing effect\>\"",
      StripOnInput->False,
      FontWeight->Bold]},
    {"\"\<Off\>\"", "\"\<off\>\"", "\"\<passive\>\"", "\"\<allowed (baseline)\
\>\""},
    {"\"\<ReadOnly (default)\>\"", "\"\<on\>\"", "\"\<pre-filter\>\"", \
"\"\<blocked by the lock\>\""},
    {"\"\<AskForWrites\>\"", "\"\<on\>\"", "\"\<pre-filter\>\"", \
"\"\<refused, approval required\>\""},
    {"\"\<ContainmentOnly\>\"", "\"\<on\>\"", "\"\<disabled\>\"", \
"\"\<blocked by the lock alone\>\""}
   },
   AutoDelete->False,
   BaseStyle->{FontSize -> 11},
   GridBoxAlignment->{"Columns" -> {{Left}}},
   GridBoxBackground->{
    "Columns" -> {{None}}, "RowsIndexed" -> {1 -> GrayLevel[0.92]}},
   GridBoxFrame->{"Columns" -> {{True}}, "Rows" -> {{True}}},
   GridBoxItemSize->{"Columns" -> {{Automatic}}, "Rows" -> {{Automatic}}},
   GridBoxSpacings->{"Columns" -> {{2}}, "Rows" -> {{1}}}],
  "Grid"]], "Text",ExpressionUUID->"0c04b121-9056-fc42-bef6-9d93afc97a13"],

Cell["The evidence", "Section",ExpressionUUID->"c81cb18e-4be3-8740-bddd-89119b65a1bd"],

Cell["\<\
The gap test sends one call per known effect over raw MCP with no client \
approval layer at all, which is the already-clicked-always-allow case. Under \
ReadOnly, DELETE, UPDATE, DeleteFile, RunProcess, URLRead and a dynamic-eval \
variant were all blocked with zero state change while the legitimate SELECT \
still returned 250. Under Off the same calls executed, proving the harness \
can really run them, so the ReadOnly blocks are the guard and not an \
inability to run.\
\>", "Text",ExpressionUUID->"47efc3d2-6235-6d48-84a3-6bf68de2e2ac"],

Cell["\<\
The containment proof is the result that answers the objection that this is \
just a denylist. The same 19 evasion payloads, which split and comment and \
dynamically assemble their SQL, alias and hide their file writes, delete \
through JDBC, and spawn processes, run under three policies on fresh \
fixtures, with state re-snapshotted independently afterwards.\
\>", "Text",ExpressionUUID->"5110b986-f83f-ea40-b15d-0f8db3a3b5f0"],

Cell[BoxData[
 TagBox[GridBox[{
    {
     StyleBox["\"\<Policy\>\"",
      StripOnInput->False,
      FontWeight->Bold], 
     StyleBox["\"\<DB inactive (min/max of 19)\>\"",
      StripOnInput->False,
      FontWeight->Bold], 
     StyleBox["\"\<research files\>\"",
      StripOnInput->False,
      FontWeight->Bold], 
     StyleBox["\"\<EVIL_ files\>\"",
      StripOnInput->False,
      FontWeight->Bold], 
     StyleBox["\"\<fixtures changed\>\"",
      StripOnInput->False,
      FontWeight->Bold]},
    {"\"\<Off\>\"", "\"\<0 / 250\>\"", "\"\<186 / 190\>\"", "\"\<5\>\"", \
"\"\<16 / 19 (payloads are real)\>\""},
    {"\"\<ReadOnly\>\"", "\"\<250 / 250\>\"", "\"\<190 / 190\>\"", \
"\"\<0\>\"", "\"\<0 / 19\>\""},
    {"\"\<ContainmentOnly (classifier off)\>\"", "\"\<250 / 250\>\"", \
"\"\<190 / 190\>\"", "\"\<0\>\"", "\"\<0 / 19\>\""}
   },
   AutoDelete->False,
   BaseStyle->{FontSize -> 11},
   GridBoxAlignment->{"Columns" -> {{Left}}},
   GridBoxBackground->{
    "Columns" -> {{None}}, "RowsIndexed" -> {1 -> GrayLevel[0.92]}},
   GridBoxFrame->{"Columns" -> {{True}}, "Rows" -> {{True}}},
   GridBoxItemSize->{"Columns" -> {{Automatic}}, "Rows" -> {{Automatic}}},
   GridBoxSpacings->{"Columns" -> {{2}}, "Rows" -> {{1}}}],
  "Grid"]], "Text",ExpressionUUID->"1dc866c5-8767-d440-927c-3d8f748467f2"],

Cell["\<\
readonly_zero_state_change:             true
containment_only_classifier_all_allow:  true
containment_only_zero_state_change:     true
containment_only_bypasses:              {}\
\>", "Program",ExpressionUUID->"e2a22d92-b99c-e741-b868-dee7052d9bcd"],

Cell["\<\
Read the third policy again. With the classifier disabled, every payload \
waved through, all seven JDBC deletes included, not one of the 19 fixtures \
changed. The read-only guarantee is the containment, not an enumeration of \
bad symbols. The denylist could have had a hole in it and the data still \
would not have moved. And it is not collateral damage to real work: under the \
same lock a legitimate SELECT of inactive rows returns 250 while a DELETE on \
the same connection fails.\
\>", "Text",ExpressionUUID->"8264d668-bf54-2541-a136-f28091851de1"],

Cell["\<\
Writing the battery also caught a genuine bug. One payload, With[{g = \
DeleteFile}, g[path]], was allowed, because DeleteFile appears as a value \
bound to g rather than as a call head, and the classifier scanned heads only. \
The fix tests every symbol, not just heads. This is exactly why the guarantee \
was moved onto containment: any denylist will have an edge like this one, so \
the design must not depend on the list being complete, and now it does not.\
\>", "Text",ExpressionUUID->"d82e7787-33da-4647-b84a-6e9aa51c8811"],

Cell["Honest scope", "Section",ExpressionUUID->"316bc6ae-10a0-6d44-830f-a1c9957ce052"],

Cell["\<\
The data-integrity guarantee is containment, not a completeness claim about \
the classifier. With the lock on, no write to the locked data succeeds \
regardless of primitive, shown with the classifier fully disabled and JDBC \
included. It does not claim that the classifier catches every technique, \
which it no longer needs to, or that containment reaches past the paths that \
are locked.\
\>", "Item",ExpressionUUID->"51eb74d1-e097-e64d-9d7e-0c158f26a0cf"],

Cell["\<\
The lock is a same-user ACL, not a separate principal. It denies writes to \
the current user, and the evaluator runs as that user, so a single payload \
that both spawned a process to rewrite the ACL and then wrote would defeat \
it. That is why the classifier still blocks the process axis, and why the \
honest production design runs the evaluator as a lower-privileged principal, \
a restricted user, a container, or a protected child kernel, that cannot \
change the ACL at all. The icacls command is the Windows mechanism; a \
read-only bind-mount or a restricted user is the equivalent elsewhere.\
\>", "Item",ExpressionUUID->"eaebc884-eb8d-8d48-973f-060429958fc8"],

Cell["\<\
It confines writes, not reads. A permitted read still runs with the user's \
rights. WolframGuard stops state change, and stops outbound network through \
the offline kernel and the classifier, but it is not a full OS sandbox that \
would also confine what an allowed read can see.\
\>", "Item",ExpressionUUID->"7d22a0cc-a2aa-8041-b5e1-ec16855ff4bc"],

Cell["\<\
It gates one entry point, the evaluator's single evaluation entry, and the \
lock protects the paths you name. Other AgentTools tools still rely on the \
client. The evaluator is the sharpest edge, arbitrary code, which is why it \
came first.\
\>", "Item",ExpressionUUID->"f449a3b3-d77c-194b-b648-7565e6e96818"],

Cell["\<\
AskForWrites has no human-in-the-loop yet, and blocks-and-labels in headless \
runs.\
\>", "Item",ExpressionUUID->"3fb0b3ec-5264-804d-982a-9f9680633f5f"],

Cell["Where it goes next", "Section",ExpressionUUID->"4a7da83d-2026-0b4e-bf75-f932e222ec06"],

Cell["\<\
A lower-privileged principal or protected child kernel, so the evaluator \
simply lacks write rights and no in-kernel trick can regain them, which also \
confines reads. This closes the one residual above.\
\>", "Item",ExpressionUUID->"eb8a25c5-95f0-ed49-a802-b8b77e62edc0"],

Cell["\<\
Grow the battery with more payload classes. Each one either passes, and we \
harden, or fails, and the claim broadens.\
\>", "Item",ExpressionUUID->"31430014-da12-da4c-b66d-08901df8dab6"],

Cell["\<\
Real AskForWrites elicitation through the MCP server-initiated request path.\
\>", "Item",ExpressionUUID->"ce309eaf-ee57-674b-8240-a01d3fc34873"],

Cell["Gate the other AgentTools tools with the same discipline.", "Item",ExpressionUUID->"8fc1f9c7-c332-0f49-8059-58398bff75c6"],

Cell["Reproduce", "Section",ExpressionUUID->"d178e6df-06df-844f-b2d0-df01cbe627a3"],

Cell["\<\
No model, no client and no network are needed for the core result.\
\>", "Text",ExpressionUUID->"3b95131a-d043-2844-aa39-9fdeddf4b72f"],

Cell["\<\
python wolframguard-poc/instrumentation/wolframguard_bypass_tests.py --run-id \
wolframguard-containment-new
python wolframguard-poc/results/wolframguard-containment-001/probe/probe_\
containment.py
python wolframguard-poc/instrumentation/wolframguard_gap_tests.py --run-id \
wolframguard-gap-new\
\>", "Program",ExpressionUUID->"3907a505-ee09-ba4d-8cc0-66e66a2d60a5"],

Cell["\<\
Then open results/wolframguard-containment-new/summary.json, confirm that \
containment_only_zero_state_change is true, and re-snapshot the \
containmentonly fixture directories by hand: every one must be 1000 by 250 \
with 10 files and no EVIL_ files. The run needs a non-administrator Windows \
account, Wolfram 15.0.1 with AgentTools 2.2.0, and a free loopback port 8765.\
\
\>", "Text",ExpressionUUID->"893aed42-d72c-5544-b399-ba6a85c8198c"],

Cell[BoxData[
 TagBox[GridBox[{
    {
     StyleBox["\"\<Path\>\"",
      StripOnInput->False,
      FontWeight->Bold], 
     StyleBox["\"\<What it is\>\"",
      StripOnInput->False,
      FontWeight->Bold]},
    {"\"\<wolframguard/WolframGuard.wl\>\"", "\"\<Pre-filter: \
parse-without-eval effect classifier and policy\>\""},
    {"\"\<wolframguard/guarded_server.wl\>\"", "\"\<Launcher: installs the \
gate, applies the OS lock, starts the real server\>\""},
    {"\"\<instrumentation/wolframguard_bypass_tests.py\>\"", "\"\<The \
three-policy containment battery\>\""},
    {"\"\<results/wolframguard-containment-001/\>\"", "\"\<Evidence: verdict, \
per-policy fixtures, and the sandbox-versus-lock probe\>\""}
   },
   AutoDelete->False,
   BaseStyle->{FontSize -> 11},
   GridBoxAlignment->{"Columns" -> {{Left}}},
   GridBoxBackground->{
    "Columns" -> {{None}}, "RowsIndexed" -> {1 -> GrayLevel[0.92]}},
   GridBoxFrame->{"Columns" -> {{True}}, "Rows" -> {{True}}},
   GridBoxItemSize->{"Columns" -> {{Automatic}}, "Rows" -> {{Automatic}}},
   GridBoxSpacings->{"Columns" -> {{2}}, "Rows" -> {{1}}}],
  "Grid"]], "Text",ExpressionUUID->"b470222e-153c-7a45-b7f4-05bb385403f7"],

Cell["The pitch", "Section",ExpressionUUID->"a20ebcb6-9061-c747-bf48-53fe5cafadfc"],

Cell["\<\
Give an AI agent a code evaluator and you have given a prompt injection a \
shell. On Wolfram/AgentTools the only thing between an injected DELETE and \
your data is one approval click, and the vendor's own documentation names \
that click as the mitigation. WolframGuard adds a second line inside the \
server. For a read-only task it places an operating-system read-only lock on \
your data, so a write fails at the OS no matter what the code does, and we \
measured it stopping even the JDBC database delete that a Wolfram sandbox \
lets through, with a parse-before-run classifier in front as a fast \
pre-filter. It is not a denylist you have to keep complete: switch the \
classifier off entirely and, across 19 evasion payloads, not one byte of \
synthetic state changes. Two files, no change to the paclet you already have, \
and every number here is one command away from being re-checked.\
\>", "Text",ExpressionUUID->"88f9000a-f2d1-364c-950f-04b51cc250b7"],

Cell["Links", "Section",ExpressionUUID->"a04d6e45-cb2d-1543-8039-85ee67812776"],

Cell["\<\
Wolfram AgentTools documentation, Security Considerations:  \
https://github.com/WolframResearch/AgentTools/blob/v2.2.0/docs/quickstart-\
coding.md#security-considerations\
\>", "Item",ExpressionUUID->"d6a1d662-d48f-0540-898e-fab196282548"],

Cell["\<\
Source code and reproduction:  https://github.com/itsalissonsilva/WolframGuard\
\>", "Item",ExpressionUUID->"adefdad6-880d-434f-8b67-0644f19833a3"]
},
